#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""depth — Ousterhout's red flags, mechanically.

    Shallow Module · Information Leakage · Overexposure ·
    Pass-Through Method · Pass-Through Variables ·
    Comment Repeats Code · Implementation Documentation Contaminates Interface

The book argues design by taste, and a taste is not a gate. But nine of its
fourteen red flags are decidable from the source text, and those nine are the
ones that recur most in machine-written code. Four are heuristics on prose:

  shallow-method                  a one-statement method: the interface is not
                                  simpler than the implementation (ch. 4.5)
  pass-through                    the body forwards to a method of the same
                                  name, adding nothing (ch. 7.1)
  comment-repeats-code            the comment's words are the code's words, so
                                  it can be written without reading anything
                                  but the line below it (ch. 13.2)
  implementation-narrative-comment  a declaration comment that narrates how
                                  the code works instead of what it promises
                                  (ch. 13.5)

Five are facts about a pair of modules, read from the import graph and the git
history rather than from a line:

  leaked-literal                  the same string, regex or magic number written
                                  in two modules (ch. 2.2, 5.3, 5.5)
  leaked-shape                    the same field list declared in two modules
                                  (ch. 2.2, 5.3, 5.5)
  co-change-without-import        two files that keep changing together with no
                                  dependency between them (ch. 5.2)
  needless-export                 exported from a module that has importers,
                                  named by none of them (ch. 4.6, 7.5)
  forwarded-parameter             a parameter never read, only forwarded down a
                                  chain of three or more (ch. 7.5)

What this tool is not: a substitute for the flags that are judgements about
meaning — temporal decomposition, conjoined methods, special/general mixture,
repetition, vague names, nonobvious code. A metric that guesses at them
produces findings nobody believes, and a gate nobody believes gets muted.
Those belong to a reviewer that reads, and the two meet in the same findings
format so one ledger can hold both (see jo-8).

The prose flags are heuristics, so the gate is a *ratchet*: give it a
baseline and it fails only on a finding that is not in the baseline. Day one on
an existing tree is therefore free — accept today's debt with
--update-baseline, and the gate's job becomes "never worse than yesterday".

Every finding carries its repair menu (--explain <flag>), so the moves are the
detector's data rather than a paragraph someone has to remember.

Usage:
    depth                       # report ./src (or .), exit 0
    depth src lib               # specific paths
    depth --baseline .depth-baseline.json   # ratchet: fail on a new finding
    depth --update-baseline     # accept today's findings as the baseline
    depth --changed             # only findings in files changed vs --base
    depth --json                # machine-readable, with each finding's moves
    depth --explain shallow-method          # the ways to fix it, and when
    depth report                # the six readings, never fails a build
    depth report --record       # append a dated snapshot, so a trend exists

Exit status: 1 when a baseline was given and a finding is not in it; 2 on error
(no baseline file, no source, lizard missing); 0 otherwise.
"""

